            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %strong clusters
            at a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            if every neighbourhood of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            intersects every element of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            The concepts of continuous function and such conditions as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compactness
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorffness
            may be defined quite nicely in terms of the convergence relation. In fact, everything about topological spaces may be defined in terms of the convergence relation, although not always nicely. This is because topological spaces form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of the category of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence+spaces"} convergence spaces
          %p
            In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is
            %strong Cauchy
            if it has elements of arbitrarily small diameter. Then a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequence"} Cauchy sequence
            iff its eventuality filter is Cauchy. (This can be generalised to
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+spaces"} uniform spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+space"} Cauchy space
